V(T_PH_CLASS, IO_A,
  "A 49-year-old woman with idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension faints while climbing two flights of stairs. Echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle, a septum bowing into the left ventricle and normal left ventricular contractility. What explains her exertional syncope?",
  [["Impaired left ventricular filling",
    "Correct. In advanced pulmonary hypertension the dilated right ventricle pushes the septum into the left ventricle, so the left ventricle fills poorly in diastole and output falls, even though its contractility is normal. Syncope on exertion is ominous: it signals a fixed low output that cannot rise with effort."],
   ["Weak left ventricular contraction",
    "Her left ventricular contractility is normal on echocardiography. Tell: the left ventricle fails to fill because the septum bows into it; its squeeze is not the problem.", "ruled out by a stated finding"],
   ["Right ventricular hypertrophy alone",
    "Hypertrophy is the early compensation that keeps output up; syncope comes later, when the ventricle dilates and fails and the septum shifts. Tell: her right ventricle is already dilated, the stage of ventricular interdependence.", "wrong stage or severity"],
   ["A drop in pulmonary resistance",
    "Pulmonary resistance is high in pulmonary arterial hypertension, not falling. Tell: her output is fixed and low because the right ventricle pumps against that resistance and the bowed septum limits left ventricular filling.", "reversed direction or value"]],
  CS(20, 21), "complication", "complication"),

